{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>I.</b> [Meaning:] 'I who [am I],' [and then separately] 'Esau your firstborn.' Others say he said 'I' in a low voice and raised his voice on the word 'Esau your firstborn.' These are words of wind. For the prophets divide into two parts: the first part, an agent by command; the second part, prophets of the future. And if they need to say something improper, it does no harm. But an agent — it is impossible that he lie at all. Now David, concerning whom it is written 'a man of Elohim' (2 Chronicles 8:14), and who said 'The spirit of YHWH spoke through me' (2 Samuel 23:2) — confused his words with Ahimelech and said 'the young men's vessels are holy' (1 Samuel 21:6) for the need of the moment. Likewise Elisha, who said to Hazael 'Go, say to him: You shall live' (2 Kings 8:10) — even though his [Hazael's] meaning was 'you shall live from this illness,' HaShem showed me that he will kill. So too Micaiah spoke a vain prayer, 'Go up and succeed' (1 Kings 22:15), as a way of rebuke. And so Daniel said, 'The dream be to your enemies' (Daniel 4:16) — because he stood before HaShem, by way of derash. And likewise Abraham said 'and indeed she is my sister' (Genesis 20:12) [and] 'we will worship and return' (Genesis 22:5)." "<b>that I may eat of his venison.</b> Like 'remember me, my Elohim, for good' (Nehemiah 5:19). And the alef is a root letter."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "Resolved האל״ף as alef by context; the comment identifies it as a root letter in ואכלה. Also resolved דה״ב as 2 Chronicles and ש״א as 1 Samuel by the cited chapter-verse numbers."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:46:06.454347686Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}